From: thingino-firmware sigmastar fork
Subject: [PATCH] infinity6b0: size the 64MB tier's mma carveout for userspace

board_late_init sets memlx/memsz from detected DRAM, and the generated
bootargs reference those variables rather than literals, so one image serves
every DRAM population of this SoC. This changes what the 64MB tier reserves:

    mma_heap sz  0x2000000 (32MB)  ->  0x1800000 (24MB)

The carveout is reserved out of LX_MEM, not alongside it -- the two figures
in /proc/cmdline are not additive. On a 64MB part that makes the split:

    LX_MEM=0x3FE0000     63.9MB   all Linux is told about
      mma_heap                    24.0MB   vendor multimedia pool
      cma                          2.0MB
      kernel image + misc          4.4MB
      -> userspace                         33.4MB   (32MB pool left 25.4MB)

25.4MB carries the streamer alone and nothing else; a web UI session on top
is what it cannot absorb. The 8MB is not free -- it is charged against the
video profile, which must be sized to fit whatever remains. Buffer cost
scales with resolution and is independent of fps, so the pool constrains
geometry rather than frame rate.

THE 128MB AND 256MB TIERS ARE LEFT ALONE ON PURPOSE. They have no shortage
to relieve, and capping their pool at 24MB would limit what those boards can
encode for no gain. Anything applied here must stay inside the 64MB branch.

A 64MB board that would rather have the stock pool than the headroom -- one
running a large main stream with no web UI -- wants this patch dropped, not
edited: 0x2000000 is the vendor's value for the tier.

Scoped to this family by the file it patches. arch/arm/cpu/armv7/Makefile
builds infinity6b0/ only under CONFIG_INFINITY6B0, and each Infinity6 family
carries its own chip.c, so no other SigmaStar target compiles this code.

--- a/arch/arm/cpu/armv7/infinity6b0/chip.c
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu/armv7/infinity6b0/chip.c
@@ -315,7 +315,7 @@
         setenv("memsz", "0x4600000");
     } else {
         setenv("memlx", "0x3FE0000");
-        setenv("memsz", "0x2000000");
+        setenv("memsz", "0x1800000");
     }

     return 0;
